    "intro": "February 2 on the Catholic calendar centers on Presentation of the Lord — Feast of Light in the Temple. Mary and Joseph presented the infant Jesus in the Temple forty days after his birth. This is not merely a historical anniversary but a solemn proclamation of faith celebrated in every Roman Rite parish that keeps the General Roman Calendar. The sections below treat Scripture, doctrine, liturgy, and family observance separately so each adds new information.",
    "earlyLife": "The prophet Simeon recognized Jesus as the light to the nations. Also called Candlemas; candles are blessed on this feast. The date February 2 places this mystery in the Church's annual cycle so believers rehearse salvation history rather than reading it once and moving on. Lex orandi, lex credendi — the way the Church prays on this day is the way she teaches what she believes.",
    "vocationAndMinistry": "Described in Luke 2:22–40 as a fulfillment of Mosaic law. Theology here is doxology: Catholics praise God for what he has done, not only study it. Preachers on February 2 connect this feast to baptism, Eucharist, and moral life — showing that liturgy and ethics are one piece. Feast of Light in the Temple gives catechists a single sentence children can remember long after details fade.",
    "historicalContext": "Parishes mark February 2 with proper readings, prayers, and often festive music when rubrics allow. Check your parish bulletin for Mass times; solemnities may include Gloria, Creed, and extended processions or blessings. In the United States, when this date is a Holy Day of Obligation, Catholics plan travel and work schedules around Mass — a countercultural witness in itself.",
