    "intro": "March 19 on the Catholic calendar centers on St. Joseph — Patron of the Universal Church and Workers. Foster father of Jesus and husband of the Blessed Virgin Mary. This is not merely a historical anniversary but a solemn proclamation of faith celebrated in every Roman Rite parish that keeps the General Roman Calendar. The sections below treat Scripture, doctrine, liturgy, and family observance separately so each adds new information.",
    "earlyLife": "Patron of the Universal Church declared by Pope Pius IX in 1870. Named patron of workers by Pope Pius XII in 1955. The date March 19 places this mystery in the Church's annual cycle so believers rehearse salvation history rather than reading it once and moving on. Lex orandi, lex credendi — the way the Church prays on this day is the way she teaches what she believes.",
    "vocationAndMinistry": "His feast on March 19 has been celebrated since the tenth century. Theology here is doxology: Catholics praise God for what he has done, not only study it. Preachers on March 19 connect this feast to baptism, Eucharist, and moral life — showing that liturgy and ethics are one piece. Patron of the Universal Church and Workers gives catechists a single sentence children can remember long after details fade.",
    "historicalContext": "Parishes mark March 19 with proper readings, prayers, and often festive music when rubrics allow. Check your parish bulletin for Mass times; solemnities may include Gloria, Creed, and extended processions or blessings. In the United States, when this date is a Holy Day of Obligation, Catholics plan travel and work schedules around Mass — a countercultural witness in itself.",
    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Home customs on March 19 should echo the sanctuary: Scripture before meals, candles, hymns, or charitable giving tied to the mystery celebrated. Ethnic parishes enrich the feast with foods and processions; the unity of faith expresses itself in legitimate diversity. Avoid reducing the day to sentiment alone — the Church calls for conversion, joy, and mission flowing from what God has revealed.",
    "patronages": "Devotion on this day often entrusts workers, fathers, the Universal Church, and a happy death to the intercession of St. Joseph and the whole communion of saints. Pray the Collect of the day from the Roman Missal — it condenses the Church's intention in authoritative language. Families can bless children, renew baptismal promises, or read the Gospel account associated with this feast before bedtime.",
    "legacy": "Every generation re-encounters St. Joseph on March 19 with new questions — suffering, hope, family fracture, or cultural hostility to faith. The feast answers by pointing to God's action, not human achievement. That is why calendar feasts remain among the most durable teachers in Catholic life: they return whether or not smartphones remind us.",
    "howToHonorToday": "Begin with Mass when obligation or schedule allows; arrive early for silence before the opening hymn. Read the day's Gospel the night before and discuss one phrase at table — formation beats elaborate programs. Extend celebration through the octave or season when rubrics provide one; do not collapse the mystery into a single hour. Perform one work of mercy: visit the sick, donate food, or forgive a family grudge as a living response to the feast.",
