    "intro": "June 24 on the Catholic calendar centers on St. John the Baptist — Forerunner Who Baptized Christ in the Jordan. Forerunner of Christ who baptized Jesus in the Jordan River. This is not merely a historical anniversary but a solemn proclamation of faith celebrated in every Roman Rite parish that keeps the General Roman Calendar. The sections below treat Scripture, doctrine, liturgy, and family observance separately so each adds new information.",
    "earlyLife": "Preached repentance in the wilderness and prepared the way of the Lord. Beheaded by Herod Antipas at the request of Salome. The date June 24 places this mystery in the Church's annual cycle so believers rehearse salvation history rather than reading it once and moving on. Lex orandi, lex credendi — the way the Church prays on this day is the way she teaches what she believes.",
    "vocationAndMinistry": "Only saint besides Mary whose birth and death are both celebrated. Theology here is doxology: Catholics praise God for what he has done, not only study it. Preachers on June 24 connect this feast to baptism, Eucharist, and moral life — showing that liturgy and ethics are one piece. Forerunner Who Baptized Christ in the Jordan gives catechists a single sentence children can remember long after details fade.",
    "historicalContext": "Parishes mark June 24 with proper readings, prayers, and often festive music when rubrics allow. Check your parish bulletin for Mass times; solemnities may include Gloria, Creed, and extended processions or blessings. In the United States, when this date is a Holy Day of Obligation, Catholics plan travel and work schedules around Mass — a countercultural witness in itself.",
    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Home customs on June 24 should echo the sanctuary: Scripture before meals, candles, hymns, or charitable giving tied to the mystery celebrated. Ethnic parishes enrich the feast with foods and processions; the unity of faith expresses itself in legitimate diversity. Avoid reducing the day to sentiment alone — the Church calls for conversion, joy, and mission flowing from what God has revealed.",
    "patronages": "Devotion on this day often entrusts baptism, conversion, monks, and Jordan to the intercession of St. John the Baptist and the whole communion of saints. Pray the Collect of the day from the Roman Missal — it condenses the Church's intention in authoritative language. Families can bless children, renew baptismal promises, or read the Gospel account associated with this feast before bedtime.",
    "legacy": "Every generation re-encounters St. John the Baptist on June 24 with new questions — suffering, hope, family fracture, or cultural hostility to faith. The feast answers by pointing to God's action, not human achievement. That is why calendar feasts remain among the most durable teachers in Catholic life: they return whether or not smartphones remind us.",
    "howToHonorToday": "Begin with Mass when obligation or schedule allows; arrive early for silence before the opening hymn. Read the day's Gospel the night before and discuss one phrase at table — formation beats elaborate programs. Extend celebration through the octave or season when rubrics provide one; do not collapse the mystery into a single hour. Perform one work of mercy: visit the sick, donate food, or forgive a family grudge as a living response to the feast.",
