    "intro": "August 15 on the Catholic calendar centers on Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary — Feast of Mary's Bodily Assumption into Heaven. Mary was taken body and soul into heavenly glory at the end of her earthly life. This is not merely a historical anniversary but a solemn proclamation of faith celebrated in every Roman Rite parish that keeps the General Roman Calendar. The sections below treat Scripture, doctrine, liturgy, and family observance separately so each adds new information.",
    "earlyLife": "Defined as dogma by Pope Pius XII in 1950. Ancient feast celebrated in both East and West since the early centuries. The date August 15 places this mystery in the Church's annual cycle so believers rehearse salvation history rather than reading it once and moving on. Lex orandi, lex credendi — the way the Church prays on this day is the way she teaches what she believes.",
    "vocationAndMinistry": "Foreshadows the resurrection of all believers at the end of time. Theology here is doxology: Catholics praise God for what he has done, not only study it. Preachers on August 15 connect this feast to baptism, Eucharist, and moral life — showing that liturgy and ethics are one piece. Feast of Mary's Bodily Assumption into Heaven gives catechists a single sentence children can remember long after details fade.",
    "historicalContext": "Parishes mark August 15 with proper readings, prayers, and often festive music when rubrics allow. Check your parish bulletin for Mass times; solemnities may include Gloria, Creed, and extended processions or blessings. In the United States, when this date is a Holy Day of Obligation, Catholics plan travel and work schedules around Mass — a countercultural witness in itself.",
    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Home customs on August 15 should echo the sanctuary: Scripture before meals, candles, hymns, or charitable giving tied to the mystery celebrated. Ethnic parishes enrich the feast with foods and processions; the unity of faith expresses itself in legitimate diversity. Avoid reducing the day to sentiment alone — the Church calls for conversion, joy, and mission flowing from what God has revealed.",
    "patronages": "Devotion on this day often entrusts heaven, the dying, and gardeners to the intercession of Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary and the whole communion of saints. Pray the Collect of the day from the Roman Missal — it condenses the Church's intention in authoritative language. Families can bless children, renew baptismal promises, or read the Gospel account associated with this feast before bedtime.",
    "legacy": "Every generation re-encounters Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary on August 15 with new questions — suffering, hope, family fracture, or cultural hostility to faith. The feast answers by pointing to God's action, not human achievement. That is why calendar feasts remain among the most durable teachers in Catholic life: they return whether or not smartphones remind us.",
    "howToHonorToday": "Begin with Mass when obligation or schedule allows; arrive early for silence before the opening hymn. Read the day's Gospel the night before and discuss one phrase at table — formation beats elaborate programs. Extend celebration through the octave or season when rubrics provide one; do not collapse the mystery into a single hour. Perform one work of mercy: visit the sick, donate food, or forgive a family grudge as a living response to the feast.",
